<p>
My new job let me choose between a Windows or Mac laptop.
As much as I dislike Apple, I despise Microsoft with all my being, so the Macbook was an obvious winner.
However, one problem with using a Macbook is that the keyboards, for me at least, are incredibly uncomfortable to use.
Maybe it's because of the short key travel or what-have-you, but keep in mind I spend about 50% of my time on a Framework and have nothing but praise for its keyboard.
</p>

<p>
After about a week of using the M1 Mac, I started to get strain in my left hand.
Part of this is undoubtedly going to be Emacs' RSI kicking in, since I still use the standard bindings for Control and Alt, instead of using Caps-Lock for Control.
I'm more willing to blame the keyboard though, since I've never had this problem before and 6 hours of programming at home at my desktop doesn't lead to any strain.
</p>

<p>
So I elected to buy a keyboard specifically for work.
I had a couple of requirements:
</p>
<ol class="org-ol">
<li>It needs to be mechanical, because membrane feels horrible and I may as well go all out.</li>
<li>It needs to be low-profile to comfortably fit in my backpack.</li>
<li>It needs to handle both wired and wireless connections.</li>
</ol>

<div class="outline-2">
<h2 id="available-options">Available Options</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2">
<p>
Pickings are a bit slim when it comes to low profile boards, especially because I just want something that works and not have to do heaps of assembly.
</p>

<p>
I specifically looked at Keychron, as I already have an older K3 and it's great, but I wanted to try out some other company and experiment a little.
Though I think there will be a time for me to try theirs again at some point.
</p>

<p>
The other board I looked at was the Flow Lite 84 from Lofree, but currency conversion and shipping costs to Australia made it prohibitively expensive.
</p>

<p>
The Node 75 from Nuphy clicked with me immediately.
I like gimmicks like the touch bar, and I like the beige option they have by default, so those are both pluses to consider.
The 3D printing extension gimmick is fun too, as is the dot matrix LED status display.
It's also cheaper than the other options at only $139 AUD, which is the reason I ultimately decided to buy it.
</p>
</div>
</div>

<div class="outline-2">
<h2 id="first-impressions-and-customer-support">First Impressions and Customer Support</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2">
<p>
My first impressions were&#x2026; Not great.
16 switches were faulty out of the box and either dropping or duplicating input, but their customer service was quick in sending replacements.
Once the replacements went in, 2 more switches shit the bed, but support covered those too.
</p>

<p>
I do really like the typing feel of the board, though I think I still prefer high profile if not for the portability issue.
I also really like that it has both bluetooth, wired, and 2.4ghz connection modes.
I primarily use it wired (out of habit, I typically don't like bluetooth preipherals) but the 2.4ghz dongle is excellent for connecting it to random devices I have lying around.
</p>

<p>
I will say that the airfeet design, that it can sit on top of the Macbook keyboard, is nice in theory but doesn't work great in practice, unless you're using it wirelessly.
The cable gets in the way of the screen, and it feels pretty cramped.
It might be nicer if the keyboard sat over the touchpad instead, since I don't really want to use the touchpad anyway.
</p>
</div>
</div>

<div class="outline-2">
<h2 id="annoyances">Annoyances</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2">
<p>
The first and most annoying thing is that the board isn't powered by open firmware.
I don't super care about this, I don't like using QMK CLI anyway, but it would be nice to have the option instead of being locked into Nuphy configurator webapp.
</p>

<p>
The touchbar, a fun gimmick though it is, also has some sensitivity problems.
It doesn't handle triple tapping properly for me which is a shame, since macros are assignable to it, and I could have it open transient menus in Emacs that way.
</p>

<p>
The other concern I have currently is that I've had the "1" key duplicate inputs once or twice now, so another switch may be on the way out&#x2026;
Or perhaps I just need to re-seat it, at the moment I'm not sure.
</p>
</div>
</div>

<div class="outline-2">
<h2 id="overall">Overall</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2">
<p>
Overall opinion is that it's a decent board with below average quality control (or I'm just unlucky).
You get what you pay for.
</p>
</div>
</div>
